�������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������\n�\n���� ��������� ���� ���� ��� ��������� ������������ ��� ������������� ����������� ��� ������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ���������������� ��� ������������ ����� �������� ����������� ���� ���������������� ������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ������� �������� ������� ������� ��������� ���������� ������� �������� ��������\n�������� �������� ������ ������ ������������ ���� ��"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|15|STARRED   |68", "t": "Development of Mavelikkara Railway Station", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 68 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2024 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF MAVELIKKARA RAILWAY STATION \n \n*68.  SHRI KODIKUNNIL SURESH: \n \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the current status of the proposed development plan under Amrit \nBharat Scheme of Mavelikkara railway station; \n \n(b) whether the Government is aware that there are reports of delay \nunder station development project in Mavelikkara citing reasons of \nunwillingness of contractors to take up the work; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and steps taken to address the issue; and \n \n(d) the details of proposed development projects for the station and \nthe estimated funds that have been allocated for the same? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n(a) to (d) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 68 BY SHRI KODIKUNNIL SURESH TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 07.02.2024 REGARDING DEVELOPMENT \nOF MAVELIKKARA RAILWAY STATION \n \n(a) to (d) Mavelikkara Railway station has been identified for \ndevelopment under Amrit Bharat Station Scheme. Presently, work has \nbeen undertaken for cover over platform, ceremonial flag, and \nprovision of lifts at platform no. 1 and 2/3. Agencies have been fixed \nfor electrical and telecom works. One contract for civil works has \nbeen terminated for non performance and the same is being \nretendered. Invitation, award and completion of contracts is an \nongoing process on Indian Railways and the same are monitored at \nvarious levels and appropriate steps are taken. \nThe redevelopment of Mavelikkara station envisages development of \nconcourse area, front porch, circulating area, parking area etc. It is \nalso planned for provision of furniture, cover over platforms, stainless \nsteel benches, wash basins, dustbins, ceremonial flag, \nelectrical/telecom arrangements etc. \nDevelopment/redevelopment/upgradation of Railway Stations is \ncomplex in nature involving safety of passengers & trains and requires \nvarious statutory clearances such as fire clearance, heritage, tree \ncutting, air-port clearance etc. The progress also gets affected due to \nbrown field related challenges such as shifting of utilities, (involving \nwater/sewage lines, optical fibre cables, gas pipe lines, power/signal \ncables etc.,) infringements, operation of trains without hindering \n\npassenger movement, speed restrictions due to works carried out in \nclose proximity of high voltage power lines etc. and these factors \naffect the completion time. Therefore, no time frame can be indicated \nat this stage. \nThe details of allocation for development and maintenance of stations \nare maintained zonal Railway-wise and not state-wise or station-wise \nor scheme-wise. Development of stations and provision of Passenger \namenities are generally funded under Plan Head – 53 ‘Customer \nAmenities’. Mavelikkara Railway Station falls under the jurisdiction of \nSouthern Railway. During the year 2023-24, a total ₹ 562.72 crore has \nbeen allocated for Southern Railway.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|15|STARRED   |69", "t": "Public Distribution System", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT  \n \n LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.69 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2024 \n  \nHOSTELS FOR WORKING WOMEN  \n  \n69.      SHRI ANTO ANTONY: \n  \n Will the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)      whether the Government is providing financial assistance for setting up of \n working women’s hostel in the country;  \n(b)      if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise including Kerala;  \n(c)      whether any applications in this regard is pending before the Government; and  \n(d)      if so, the details and the reasons therefor? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n  \n(a) to (d)     The Sakhi Niwas Scheme (Working Women Hostel) under the overarching \nMission Shakti, is a demand driven centrally sponsored scheme where States/UTs \nassess their requirement as per local needs and proposals are approved by the \nProgramme Approval Board (PAB) after discussion with States/UTs.   The Scheme \naims t o promote availability  of safe and conveniently located accommodations for \nworking women, with day care facility for their children in urban, semi urban, or even \nrural areas where employment opportunity for women exist.  The Scheme has funding \nratio of 60:40 between the Centre and the State Government and UTs with legislature \nexcept North East & Special Category States where funding ratio is 90:10.   For UTs \nwithout Legislature, 100% funding is provided by the central government.  \n  \nThe State/UT-wise details of fund released during the last three financial year \nand current financial year under Sakhi Niwas Scheme are at Annexure-I. \n \n*****  \n\nAnnexure-I \n  \nAnnexure with regard to reply to Parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred \nQuestion No.69 for answer on 02.02.2024 regarding Hostels for Working Women \nasked by Shri Anto Antony, Hon’ble Member of Parliament (LS). \n  \nState/UT-wise details of fund released during the last three financial year and \ncurrent financial year under Sakhi Niwas Scheme. \n  \n(Amount Rupees in Lakhs) \n  \n  \n****** \nStates/UTs 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 2023-24 \n(upto \n31.01.2024) \nAndhra Pradesh - - - - \nAssam 7.76 - - 34.26 \nChhattisgarh - 5.46 - 19.18 \nGujarat - 69.70 - - \nHimachal Pradesh - - - - \nKarnataka - - - - \nKerala - 273.97 384.00 48.00 \nMadhya Pradesh - 191.19 - - \nMaharashtra 36.38 - - - \nMizoram - 40.88 - - \nManipur 990.61 164.70 784.85 - \nNagaland 223.67 96.09 - 103.74 \nPunjab - - - - \nTamil Nadu 392.18 350.25 593.94 - \nTelangana - - - - \nDelhi 300.18 11.52 - - \nTotal 1950.78 1203.76 1762.79 205.18 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|15|STARRED   |7", "t": "Setting up of Medical Colleges", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 7\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 2nd FEBRUARY 2024\nSETTING UP OF HUMAN MILK BANKS\n7. SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that human milk banks are in prevalence in the country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n(c) whether the Government proposes to set up such banks for providing/supplying free of cost\nmilk to sick and prematurely born infants in the country;\n(d) if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) whether the Government has prepared any guidelines for such banks and if so, the details\nthereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY\nWELFARE (DR. BHARATI PRA VINPAWAR)\n(a) to (e): As per the National Guidelines on Lactation Management Centre (LMC) in\nPublic Health facilities, Government has established Comprehensive Lactation Management\nCentres (CLMC) to ensure availability of safe, pasteurized Donor Human Milk and Lactation\nManagement Units (LMU) for facilitating expression of mother’s own milk, for feeding of\nsick, preterm and low birth weight babies admitted in Neonatal Intensive Care Units (NICU)\nand Special Newborn Care Units (SNCU).\n\nThese guidelines provide operational and technical guidance for establishment and\nmanagement of LMCs, so that donor human milk is expressed, collected, processed and\ndispensed free of cost for feeding of infants admitted in government health facilities.\nAs per the reports received from the States/UTs for the FY 2023-24 (up to Q3), 50\nComprehensive Lactation Management Centres and 38 Lactation Management Units are\ncurrently operational across the country in 14 States/UTs. Government provides support\nthrough the National Health Mission for setting up of Lactation Management Centres based\non the proposals received from the States/UTs in their Annual Programme Implementation\nPlans.\n********"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|15|STARRED   |70", "t": "Clubbing of Non-Personal Data", "head": "�������������������� �\n�������������������������������������������������� �\n��������� �\n������������������� ������\n����������������� �������������\n��\n���������������� �������������� �\n�\n������� ���������������� �\n�\n����� ���� ��������� ��� ������������ ���� ����������������������� ��� �������� ���\n�������\n�\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������\n���������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������\n�\n������ �\n�\n�������������������������������������������������� �\n����������������������� �\n�\n��������������������������������