Construction of Tribal Girls \nHostel \nDuncan Basti, Dimapur 75.00 completed \n23.   Construction of Hostel at \nBaptist School \nBotsa, Kohima 75.00 Completed \n24.   Construction of Hostel for \nTribal Students Jakhama, Kohima \n50.00 60% completed \n25.   \nConstruction of Hostel Zapami village, Phek \n30.00 50% completed \n26.   \nConstruction of Hostel Chukitong, Wokha \n50.00 50% completed \n27.   \nConstruction of Hostel Diphupar, Dimapur \n100.00 50% completed \n28.   \nConstruction of Hostel Bade, Dimapur \n50.00 50% completed \n29.   \nConstruction of Hostel \nArtang Ward, \nMokokchung \n50.00 \nIncomplete \n30.   \nConstruction of Hostel \nBilly Graham Road, \nKohima \n50.00 70% completed \n31.   \nConstruction of Hostel Kyong Colony, Dimapur \n50.00 50% completed \n32.   \nConstruction of Hostel Signal Basti, Dimapur \n270.00 70% completed \n33.   \n Construction of Hostel Zubza, Kohima \n50.00 50% completed \n34.   \nConstruction of Hostel NST Colony, Dimapur \n130.00 \ncompleted \n35.   \nConstruction of Hostel  \nKezo Town, Chakabama, \nKohima \n100.00 \n60% completed \n36.   Construction of Tribal \nHostel  Chare, Tuensang \n150.00 \ncompleted \n37.   Construction of Hostel at \nTobu Government High \nSchool Tobu, Mon \n150.00 \nCompleted \n38.   Construction of Girls \nHostel Building   Kandinu, Tsemenyu \n100 \nCompleted \n39.   Construction of Tribal \nHostel Nuiland, Dimapur \n100 \nCompleted \n40.   \nTribal Girls Hostel Satoi, Zunheboto \n100.00 \nCompleted \n\n41.   \nWorking Women Hostel Dimapur \n90.00 \nCompleted \n42.  Tribal Hostel Hospital Colony, Kohima 150.00 Ongoing \n43.  Tribal Hostel Aboi, Mon 100.00 Incomplete \n44.  Boys & Girls hostel Angjangyang, Mon 100.00 Not complete \n45.  Tribal Boys Hostel Tseminyu, Kohima 100.00 Not complete \n46.  Hostel Bayavu Hill, Kohima 100.00 Not complete \n47.  Tribal Hostel  Sechu-Zubza 100.00 Not complete \n48.  Boys Hostel Peren Govt. College, \nPeren \n100.00 Not complete \n49.  Tribal Hostel for tribal \nStudents \nWangkhao College, Mon 100.00 Not complete \n50. "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|15|UNSTARRED |346", "t": "Venture Capital Fund to Entrepreneurs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- 346 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 05/02/2024 \n  \nVENTURE CAPITAL FUND TO ENTREPRENEURS \n \n346. SHRI S. MUNISWAMY: \n \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has allocated/distributed of funds to the entrepreneurs desiring to avail the \nfacilities of the scheme floated by the ST DFC as a venture capital fund; \n \n(b) whether Ready Mix Concrete Plants in an innovative way having social obligations and factors \nincluded under its ambit be considered as an innovative venture; \n \n(c) the details of the maximum limit of the loans under this scheme; \n \n(d) whether there is a provision for relaxation on the maximum limit to a deserving entrepreneur who \nfulfils all the parameters of the scheme as a special case; \n \n(e) the details of the officers responsible for this scheme and whether Industrial Finance Corporation \nof India (IFCI) the nodal agency which is part of the scheme in facilitating the process is empowered \nto take final decisions on the matters pertaining to this scheme; and \n \n(f) the list of eligible/shortlisted entrepreneurs/ organizations if the scheme has taken off? \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR)  \n \n(a) to (f): The government has approved a scheme of Venture Capital Fund for Scheduled Tribes (VCF-\nSTs) with the corpus of INR 50.00 crores to provide financial assistance to the ST community for \nundertaking various activities to promote entrepreneurship and innovation. Industrial Finance \nCorporation of India Venture Capital Fund Ltd. (IFCI VCF Ltd.) is the nodal agency to implement the \nscheme. The maximum limit of financial assistance to be provided under the scheme is INR 5.00 crores \nirrespective of the nature of the activity. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|15|UNSTARRED |347", "t": "Printing Discrepancies in Class 10th and 12th Certificates", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 347 \nANSWERED ON-05/02/2024 \n \nPrinting Discrepancies in Class 10th and 12th Certificates \n347. SHRI GHANSHYAM SINGH LODHI: \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the percentage of cases reported per year in the \nStates relating to the printing discrepancies such as wrong names etc. on the certificates \nissued to the students of class 10th and 12th; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the efforts made by the Government to prevent such \nerrors on the certificates; \n(c) whether the discrepancies in the data base are avoidable so as to control the \ninconvenience among students; and \n(d) whether any scheme is being run by the Department of Education, Uttar Pradesh to \nfix the time limit to rectify the printing mistakes on the certificates of students through \nonline application? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n(a) to (c): Education being a subject in the Concurrent List of the Constitution and \nmajority of schools being under the jurisdiction of the State/ Union Territory \nGovernments, it is for the respective State/ Union Territory Government to take a \ndecision in the matter for their schools. \nThe certificates are issued by the respective State/ UT Boards to the students of class \n10th and 12 th on the basis of data provided by students/ schools. As far as CBSE is \nconcerned they have informed that r egular instructions are issued  by the concerned \nBoards to schools and students to upload the correct data. Further, confirmation from the \nparents is also obtained regarding the data printed on the admit card of the students. In \ncase any request from a school is received even after submission of the list of \nCandidates, the portal for submitting the data is again opened for furnishing the updated \ncorrect information. \n(d): As per information received from Government of Uttar Pradesh, t he data of students \ngets updated  regularly on the website of U ttar Pradesh Madh yamik Shiksha  Parishad \n(UPMSP) through Principals of the Schools and a time limit of 15 days is set under the \nJanhit Guarantee Act , 2011 for rectification of errors in the certificate. Along with this \n‘SAMADHAN’ portal launched by the UP Board is also functional for online rectification of \nany error in certificates. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|15|UNSTARRED |348", "t": "GST Collection", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \n \nLOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 348 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 05, 2024 / MAGHA 16, 1945 (Saka) \n \nGST Collection \n \n348. SHRI ADHIKARI DEEPAK (DEV): \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total GST collection during the last three years; \n(b) the details of GST collection with amount during the above said period, State-wise; and  \n(c) the number of cases registered on GST fraud during the above-said period, State-wise?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) & (b): The details of gross Goods and Services Tax (GST) collection on all supplies \n(Domestic + Imports) during the last three financial years are given as per Annexure 1. \n(c): The details of number of cases registered on GST fraud by Central Tax formations during \nthe last three financial years are given as per Annexure 2. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE-1 \n  \nTotal GST Collection on all supplies (Domestic + Imports) \n Rs. In Crores \nS. No.  State/UT FY 2020-21 FY 2021-22 FY 2022-23 FY 2023-24 (Upto \nDecember, 23 \n1 Jammu and Kashmir 3648  4692 5246 5004 \n2 Himachal Pradesh 7055  8023 8778 7483 \n3 Punjab 13913  18406 20949 17827 \n4 Chandigarh 1651  1979 2365 2071 \n5 Uttarakhand 12339  13680 16845 14359 \n6 Haryana 54890  68142 86668 75478 \n7 Delhi 36568  46253 55843 49307 \n8 Rajasthan 31797  38480 45458 36640 \n9 Uttar Pradesh 59721  73865 87970 75956 \n10 Bihar 11638  13534 16548 12973 \n11 Sikkim 2266  2811 3156 2809 \n12 Arunachal Pradesh 651  710 1023 962 \n13 Nagaland 389  420 566 526 \n14 Manipur 392  551 615 489 \n15 Mizoram 257  316 419 362 \n16 Tripura 732  777 884 767 \n17 Meghalaya 1337  1764 2076 1653 \n18 Assam 10030  12007 13710 11296 \n19 West Bengal 39694  47899 58060 46318 \n20 Jharkhand 20482  27854 32019 25469 \n21 Odisha 29844  44335 49442 39540 \n22 Chhattisgarh 24419  29571 31968 25659 \n23 Madhya Pradesh 27005  31255 36232 30935 \n24 Gujarat 74346  97155 114221 91780 \n25 Daman and Diu 305  5 3 2 \n26 Dadra and Nagar Haveli 2349  3141 3771 3130 \n27 Maharashtra 165308  217993 270346 237022 \n29 Karnataka 75660  95926 122822 106117 \n30 Goa 3270  4364 5520 4742 \n31 Lakshadweep 13  18 21 40 \n32 Kerala 17349  22264 27371 22615 \n33 Tamil Nadu 69121  85492 104377 90993 \n34 Puducherry 1646  1824 2373 1947 \n35 Andaman and Nicobar Islands 255 332 373 322 \n36 Telangana 36346  45081 51831 43884 \n37 Andhra Pradesh 26163  32710 40232 32951 \n38 Ladakh 114  207 333 356 \n97 Other Territory 1337  1590 2609 2017 \n99 Center Jurisdiction 1541  2121 1941 1818 \n  Total 865842  1097545 1324985 1123618 \n  IGST on Imports 261774  376956 471799 364572 \n  Cess on Imports 9190  8789 10896 9132 \n  Grand Total 1136805  1483291 1807680 1497322 \n\nANNEXURE-2 \nGST Evasion cases registered by Central Tax formations \nS. No. State/UT \nNo. of cases registered \nFY 2020-21 FY 21-22 FY 22-23 \nFY 23-24 \n(Upto December \n2023) \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 0 0 4 0 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 229  407 234 184 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 9  5 19 125 \n4 Assam 81  256 382 413 \n5 Bihar 148  256 160 210 \n6 Chandigarh 60  71 47 30 \n7 Chhattisgarh 336  429 400 351 \n8 Daman & Diu and Dadra \nNagar Haveli 37 46 51 26 \n9 Delhi 571  456 355 407 \n10 Goa 43  48 43 70 \n11 Gujarat 2000  1987 3108 2589 \n12 Haryana 1062  644 1532 1123 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 33  124 82 83 \n14 Jammu & Kashmir 14  89 78 104 \n15 Jharkhand 221  274 281 340 \n16 Karnataka 529  735 959 673 \n17 Kerala 155  157 230 467 \n18 Ladakh 0  2 1 0 \n19 Lakshadweep 0  0 0 0 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 368  371 384 530 \n21 Maharashtra 1695  2540 2889 2716 \n22 Manipur 12  18 27 15 \n23 Meghalaya 20  23 68 65 \n24 Mizoram 14  7 7 5 \n25 Nagaland 21  7 41 42 \n26 Odisha 275  196 213 130 \n27 Puducherry 45  30 41 32 \n28 Punjab 333  138 203 138 \n29 Rajasthan 494  422 809 665 \n30 Sikkim 8  11 24 16 \n31 Tamil Nadu 2230  524 637 715 \n32 Telangana 405  457 601 501 \n33 Tripura 111  30 91 76 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 417  735 836 616 \n35 Uttarakhand 56  29 77 42 \n36 West Bengal 564  1050 648 1098 \n  Total 12596 12574 15562 14597 \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|15|UNSTARRED |349", "t": "Jan Shikshan Sansthan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 349 \nANSWERED ON 05.02.2024 \n \nJAN SHIKSHAN SANSTHAN \n349. SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL: \n                Will the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP be pleased to \nstate : \n(a) the details of the advancements achieved in broadening the coverage of the Jan Shikshan \nSansthan (JSS) scheme; \n(b) the initiatives taken by the Government to ensure that the vocational skills and training \noffered under this scheme are pertinent and in harmony with local employment prospects and \nmarket requirements; \n(c) the details of the share of women in the total beneficiaries under the scheme; and \n(d) the efforts made to integrate this scheme with other similar programs to enhance \neffectiveness and coordination? \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a)          The Jan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS) Scheme, initially launched as Shramik Vidyapeeth \n(SVP) in 1967, aims to provide skill training in a non-formal mode at the doorsteps of the \nbeneficiary through registered Societies (NGOs) with 100% grant from the Government of India. \nThe objective of the scheme is to increase the household income by promoting self/wage \nemployment through skill development training. The main target of the JSS is to impart vocational \nskills to the non-literates, neo-literates and persons having rudimentary level of education upto \n8th standard and school drop outs upto 12th standard in the age group of 15-45 years. \n \nThe Scheme was transferred from Ministry of Education to Ministry of Skill Development and \nEntrepreneurship (MSDE) in July, 2018. Since, the transfer of the Scheme, the Ministry has taken \nseveral steps in broadening its coverage. From 228 functional JSSs in 2018, the number of JSSs \nincreased to 288 in financial year 2023-24 across 26 States and 7 UTs, including aspirational \ndistricts, Left Wing Extremist areas, tribal, border and other backward districts of the country. \nFurther, various initiatives have been taken for the advancement in the coverage of the Scheme. \nThis includes- \n(i)  Age relaxation is given to SC, ST, Divyang and other deserving cases, especially women. \n(ii)   Skill training is organized with special concern for deprived sections, women/adolescent \ngirls and unemployed youth to provide skilling, up-skilling, and re-skilling for self-employment \nto enhance the family income. \n\n(iii)  The courses selected under the Scheme are based on local market demand and \nbeneficiaries’ requirements assessed through door-to-door surveys. Courses with maximum \nwage/self-employment opportunities are selected.  \n(iv) To maintain uniformity and skill standards, all the courses are aligned to National Skill \nQualification Framework (NSQF). \n(v)  Digital Literacy and employability skills have been incorporated in the course curriculum.  \n(vi)  Capacity building of JSS Staff and Entrepreneurship Development Programme for JSSs \nResource Persons. \n(vii)  Upgrading tools and equipments of model JSSs centres. \n(viii)  The National Council for Vocational Education and Training (NCVET) has approved 15 \nQualifications / Courses for the Scheme of JSS which has been aligned with NSQF levels 2 \nand 3. Further, in view of the local market demand and geographical requirements and to cater \nthe beneficiaries with suitable courses, 35 more NSQF-aligned courses of level 2 have been \nadopted for training during FY 2023-24. \n(ix) Under the Scheme, Livelihood Cells have been created to encourage the beneﬁciaries for \nself/wage employment and to assist in the mobilization of loans and formation of Self Help \nGroups (SHGs) / Joint Liability Groups and creating market linkages and promoting livelihood \nand Entrepreneurship Development. \n(x)  The Ministry has leveraged the use of technology through a specially designed web portal \nwhich aims to bring transparency and accountability, providing quality and timely information to \nall the stakeholders of the Skill Ecosystem. In the JSS Scheme, all the data including enrolled, \ntrained, assessed, certiﬁed is being captured through the JSS portal which helps in the \nsupervision and monitoring of the Scheme. \n(xi) The Ministry in order to ensure access to quality skill development, relevant opportunities \nand entrepreneurial support, has developed the Skill India Digital (SID) Portal as a \ncomprehensive digital platform aimed at synergizing and transforming the skills, education and \nentrepreneurship landscape in the country. \nSince, the transfer of the scheme from Ministry of Education to MSDE in July 2018 till \n02.01.2024, a total of 22,96,380 beneficiaries have been trained across 288 JSSs in 26 States \nand 7 UTs. The physical training achievement of the JSSs since FY 2018-19 to FY 2023-24 is \ngiven below: \nFinancial Year Enrolled Trained Assessed Certified \n2018-2019 167283 167283 158576 156639 \n2019-2020 4"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|15|UNSTARRED |35", "t": "Utilization of Nirbhaya Fund", "head": "������������������� �\n��������������������������������������� �\n��\n��������� �\n������������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n��\n���������������������������� �\n��\n������������������������� �\n�������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n����������������������������\n����� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������\n����� ���� �������� ���� ���� ������������������ ��� ������ ���������� ��� ���� ��������� ����\n����������������������������������������������������\n����� ���� ������ ������� ��������� ��� ��� ������ ��� ���� ����������� ��� �������� ����\n����������������������\n����� ������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������� ������������ ��� ���� ��������� ���������� ��� ������ ���������\n������������ �\n�\n��\n������ �\n��\n��������������������������������������� �\n����������������������������� �\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ���������� ����� ���������� ���� ��������� ���� ������������� ��������� ��� ����\n������������ ������������ ���� ��������� ������� ���� ��������� ����� ������ ���������� ��� ���\n��������� �����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ��� ������� ��������� ��� ���� ������������ ������������ ���� ��������� ���� ��� ����\n����������������������������� �\n�\n���� ��� ��������� ���������� �������� ������ ��������� ����� ���� ������� �������� ����\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������� ����� ���� �� ���